st of  the country started  I found the country was so high because  the Russians were retreating it's so  good we are Ukraine we arrived in Israel  you walk into this forest and you see  these  hundreds of crosses on top of little  mounds of sand next to a cemetery just a  few had names on them the rest were just  crosses you didn't know who they were it  was also shocking to see these poor  forensic experts digging out these  bodies a smell literally stayed under my  nose for days on end  after that each time we went to a newly  liberated area  you know you had this apprehension how  many bodies are we going to see how much  more are we going to see because it is a  terrible scene and what really surprised  me in all of that is that you had family  members you had friends who were coming  there to ask  about their loved ones and they were so  collected and so calm and so olderly and  following instructions despite you could  see all that pain etched on their face  and that's the moment I'll never ever  forget people looking for the bodies of  their loved ones regardless of how they  died is just quite shocking  I think the attacks on the big cities  the attacks on the infrastructure on the  power stations on the water supply chain  and that will continue and in all of  that you still have ukrainians who tell  you we will continue fighting even if we  have to spend the winter in the cold and  in the dark and bear in mind that a lot  of people lost winter especially the  elderly had died of cold in areas that  were under bombardment  I could not sometimes stand in the  street for more than five to eight  minutes  it's Sub-Zero during the day it could be  minus 20 degrees centigrade and then it  go down at night to minus 30 as the  colder days approach I really wonder  what ukrainians are gonna do I mean they  have small children they have elderly  parents uh vulnerable population that  cannot survive in that cold and that  really worries me a lot because I did  feel